+An **observer congruence** on an open set $U\subseteq M$ is a smooth field of future unit timelike vectors on $U$, as in [[def-gr-observer-congruence-and-adapted-charts]]; its observer curves are its integral curves. A **tetrad** on a nonempty open set $U$ means a smooth ordered frame field $(E_0,E_1,E_2,E_3)$ with $g(E_a,E_b)=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)_{ab}$ for $a,b=0,1,2,3$ and $E_0$ future-directed everywhere: $g(E_0,N)<0$ in the curved case, or $E_0(p)\in C^+$ in the flat case with $g=\eta$. This is the future-directed tetrad field of [[def-gr-local-frame-and-tetrad-fields]]. A **chart** is a coordinate map; an adapted chart is a chart whose spatial coordinates are constant along the congruence's integral curves. These objects are distinct: a chart need not be inertial, a tetrad need not be coordinate induced, and a congruence is a field of observers rather than a chart or a frame. This distinction agrees with [[def-sr-chart-tetrad-and-congruence-distinction]].
 
 The coordinates and units used later are SI: in an inertial chart $x^0=ct$ and $x^i$ are metres, $t$ is seconds, $c>0$ is metres per second, and proper time is measured in seconds. In a general chart, coordinate components inherit their coordinate units; invariant equations are tensorial. No global inertial chart, global tetrad, or global observer congruence is asserted to exist.
